Module-port source type propagation

Run after building the compiler:
  GHC=/path/to/compiler-ghc ./run-mux.sh

The direct fixture begins without evaluator observations. It checks recovery
from method input/output port types, joins of multiple interpretations, split ports,
method multiplicity, and exclusion of selectors, enables, arithmetic operands,
bare Bit port seeds, and independent unspecified defaults. Hardware fields must
remain unchanged. The BSV fixture checks declared physical wires and .ba reload.

The commands/WireTypes.bsv regression also demonstrates an incremental gain:
the rotated palette input MUX_pal$write_1__VAL_2 retains Vector#(4, Color).

Conditional source type propagation (separate step)

  GHC=/path/to/compiler-ghc ./run-conditional.sh

Candidate sets flow in both directions between selection results and their
data alternatives, including siblings and case defaults. Selectors and labels
remain separate. Array elements and dynamic selection results also share
candidates through an element group, without assigning element types to the
aggregate array itself. Mux and conditional
edges share one closure to handle alternating nests and new candidates arriving
at already visited shared values. The direct fixture checks these cases, same-
width and operation boundaries, arm-to-result and sibling inference, idempotence, and
unchanged hardware. The BSV fixture checks new types on actual physical ports
and preservation through .ba reload.

Candidate sets remain sets of IType; no provenance or serialization fields are
added. Real aliases and CSE joins union all candidates. Independent unspecified
values and unrelated literal occurrences do not connect selection groups.
Closure reruns after wire/register inlining and optimization expose new edges.

End-to-end candidate-set fixtures (default and seven backend flags):
  GHC=/path/to/compiler-ghc ./run-candidate-sets.sh

Primitive-derived numeric and Boolean candidates (separate late pass)

  GHC=/path/to/compiler-ghc ./run-primitives.sh
  BSC_BASELINE_INSTALL=/path/to/baseline/inst ./run-primitives.sh

The optional baseline compares generated Verilog byte-for-byte. All assertions
refer to physical declarations and also check serialized module reload.

Semantic Boolean operators, comparison results and selector roles supply Bool;
width one alone does not. Signed/unsigned comparisons, right shifts and unsigned
division/remainder supply numeric interpretations at each operand's own width.
Ambiguous arithmetic propagates only common, already-known Int/UInt families
(literals can take context). It does not invent signedness or transfer arbitrary
record/enum types. Numeric family rules are directional; shift counts and case
labels remain separate. Both extension variants exist for both numeric families;
truncation changes the candidate width, while arbitrary slices do not transfer a
whole-value interpretation. A full-width raw multiplier can only propagate an
already-known unsigned family, never invent a widened signed product.

The pass runs after AOpt and before primitive lowering. It uses the existing
alias/CSE/selection closure and a node-indexed numeric worklist; flattened
associative operations use binary family hubs to avoid quadratic rescans.
Derived candidates are valid operation interpretations, not proof of the user's
original source declaration. No provenance/serialization fields are added.

Late Verilog rewrite preservation (separate step):
  GHC=/path/to/compiler-ghc ./run-late-rewrite.sh

Whole-value, equal-width rewrites retain all existing candidates, including
nominal interpretations, on newly created or reused helper wires. The pure
checks cover dynamic and constant signed shifts, rewritten parents, CSE unions,
foreign arguments, unknown/literal isolation, idempotence, and metadata-independent
hardware. The BSV checks use actual emitted full-width declarations and clear/
reload the .ba for every module under default and seven backend flags.
